{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md && sed -n '1,580p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc && rg -n \\\"published-site-sets-verify-test|pym\\\\.k|yang\\\\.call|noninterruptible|site-set\\\" test src\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\n\n```sh\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\n```\n\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\n\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\n\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\n\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\n\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\n\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\navailable. **Individual tests may run on the laptop; suite-scale runs may\nnot (owner ruling 2026-11-04).** A single failing test or one namespace\nreproduces quickly and is fine locally. Everything suite-scale — subsystem\nlane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), the full quick suite, the slow tier,\n`bb test:all`, and `nightly.clj` — runs on the remote hosts: the suite has\noutgrown the laptop. The slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM\nprocesses each multi-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core\ncount); four of them GC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over\n100 on 2026-11-01 while making no progress (the remote hosts run this tier:\njupiter 5,645 s at 12 workers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane\nset concurrently on the laptop either.\n\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\n\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\n\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\n\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\n\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n(ns yang.python.antlr.siteset\n  \"C4 slice I7 (docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.6, ruling 14): published site\n   sets for imported modules.\n\n   Site marks never enter canonical rows, so a published module tree\n   carries none; they live in the frontend-metadata side table. A module's\n   site set is that side table's `:yang/site` marks for the module tree,\n   sorted (`yang.safepoint/site-set`) and content-addressed. A separate\n   publication record, keyed by the module's manifest address, pins the\n   association: the exact code root (the manifest's `:yin.module/tree`),\n   the frontend revision, the transformation profile and the set's\n   address. The module manifest keeps its schema: a manifest address names\n   code, and a record names a manifest.\n\n   Discovery. The publisher signs a publication entry per module,\n   `{:yang.site/manifest m :yang.site/record record-address ...}`, and\n   indexes it as the datoms `[e :yang.site/envelope env]` and `[e\n   :yang.site/proof proof]` beside its name envelopes (`signed-entry`).\n   A reader `discover`s a manifest's record addresses from the index\n   datoms it loaded, under the principals it declares; only a declared\n   principal's signature makes an entry count. The record and the set are\n   fetched by address and verified against it.\n\n   A consumer under a profile requiring hooks `admit`s an import: it\n   verifies every pin against the published code root and refuses a\n   missing, empty or mismatched set, naming the profile and the set.\n   `stage` then applies a verified set at link time, as the linker\n   composition's `:stage`: it derives the instrumented module `A'` from the\n   published tree alone, bottom-up over the module's guest requirements,\n   and publishes it beside its `:derive` record. An explicitly noninterruptible profile imports the module\n   as published, with none.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [yang.python.antlr.lower :as lower]\n            [yang.safepoint :as safepoint]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.encoder :as encoder]\n            [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]\n            [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]))\n\n\n(def frontend-revision\n  \"The frontend revision the marks are pinned to: it moves with any change\n   to which nodes the Python lowering marks, or how.\"\n  \"yang.python.antlr.lower/sites-1\")\n\n\n(def lowering-profile\n  \"The transformation profile the marks were made under: the module\n   emitter over the linked prelude, marking these kinds.\"\n  {:yang.site/unit :module,\n   :yang.site/prelude :linked,\n   :yang.site/kinds [:call :line :loop]})\n\n\n(def profile-address\n  \"The address the transformation profile is pinned by.\"\n  (jing/segment-key lowering-profile))\n\n\n(defn- medium\n  []\n  :yang.python.antlr/site-set)\n\n\n(defn site-set-of\n  \"`{:root address :tree tree :set [[path mark] ...]}` for the module map\n   AST `ast`: the address of its canonical tree, the tree's rows and its\n   sorted site set.\"\n  [ast]\n  (let [env (encoder/project (encoder/source-envelope\n                               (medium) [:site-set 0] [ast]))\n        tree (nth (:yin/batch env) (:yin/root env))\n        origin (vm/source-origin (:yin/source-medium env)\n                                 (:yin/batch-token env) (:yin/root env))\n        sites (safepoint/sites (:yin/frontend-metadata env) origin (:root tree))]\n    {:root (:root tree), :tree tree, :set (safepoint/site-set sites)}))\n\n\n(defn record\n  \"The publication record associating manifest `manifest` with site set\n   `set-address` over code root `root`.\"\n  [manifest root set-address]\n  {:yang.site/manifest manifest,\n   :yang.site/root root,\n   :yang.site/revision frontend-revision,\n   :yang.site/profile profile-address,\n   :yang.site/set set-address})\n\n\n(defn publish!\n  \"Publish the site set of module `ast` (published at manifest address\n   `manifest`) into `handle`: the set as one blob, then its record. Answers\n   `{:record r :record-address a :set-address s :set [...]}`.\"\n  [handle manifest ast]\n  (let [{:keys [root set]} (site-set-of ast)\n        set-address (jing/materialize! handle (vec set))\n        r (record manifest root set-address)]\n    {:record r,\n     :record-address (jing/materialize! handle r),\n     :set-address set-address,\n     :set set}))\n\n\n(defn- read-at\n  \"The value at `address` in `handle`: `::absent` when the handle holds no\n   bytes for a segment address (the handle is asked with a not-found\n   sentinel, as a byte store is), nil when `address` is no segment address\n   or its bytes do not hash to it, else the value.\"\n  [handle address]\n  (if-not (jing/segment-address? address)\n    nil\n    (let [bs ((:get-bytes-fn handle) address ::absent)]\n      (cond\n        (= ::absent bs) ::absent\n        (nil? bs) ::absent\n        (jing/segment-bytes-match? address bs) (jing/segment-value address bs)\n        :else nil))))\n\n\n(defn- absent?\n  [x]\n  (= ::absent x))\n\n\n(defn- missing\n  \"The stage's report of the absent `address`: first one met, nothing\n   written.\"\n  [address]\n  {:status :missing, :address address})\n\n\n(defn- fetch\n  \"The value at `address` in `handle`, or nil when absent or not its\n   address.\"\n  [handle address]\n  (let [v (read-at handle address)]\n    (when-not (absent? v) v)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Discovery\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn signed-entry\n  \"The publisher `key`'s (`{:seed :public}`) signed publication entry\n   `{:envelope e :proof p}` for the record at `record-address` of the\n   module at `manifest`: what the index carries so a reader discovers the\n   record from the published code.\"\n  [key manifest record-address]\n  (let [env {:yang.site/op :publish,\n             :yang.site/manifest manifest,\n             :yang.site/record record-address,\n             :yin.module/asserted-by (sign/principal (:public key))}]\n    {:envelope env, :proof (sign/sign-envelope (:seed key) env)}))\n\n\n(defn entry-datoms\n  \"The index datoms of the signed entries `signed`, entity ids from 0.\"\n  [signed]\n  (vec (mapcat (fn [e {:keys [envelope proof]}]\n                 [[e :yang.site/envelope envelope]\n                  [e :yang.site/proof proof]])\n               (range) signed)))\n\n\n(defn discover\n  \"The record addresses `datoms` (`[e a v ...]`, a loaded index's) publish\n   for the module at `manifest`, in datom order: the entries whose proof is\n   a valid signature by one of the `publics` (64 hex keys) the reader\n   declares, and no other.\"\n  [datoms publics manifest]\n  (let [proofs (reduce (fn [acc [e a v]]\n                         (cond-> acc\n                           (= a :yang.site/proof) (update e (fnil conj #{}) v)))\n                       {} datoms)]\n    (vec (distinct\n           (keep (fn [[e a env]]\n                   (when (and (= a :yang.site/envelope)\n                              (map? env)\n                              (= :publish (:yang.site/op env))\n                              (= manifest (:yang.site/manifest env)))\n                     (let [ps (get proofs e)\n                           sig (when (= 1 (count ps))\n                                 (:yin.module/signature (first ps)))]\n                       (when (and (string? sig)\n                                  (some #(sign/verify-envelope\n                                           % (jing/canonical-bytes env) sig)\n                                        publics))\n                         (:yang.site/record env)))))\n                 datoms)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Verification and admission\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- refusal\n  [reason requirement message data]\n  (merge {:status :refused,\n          :reason reason,\n          :profile (:profile requirement),\n          :message (str \"profile \" (pr-str (:profile requirement))\n                        \" requires hooks: \" message)}\n         data))\n\n\n(defn- entry-ok?\n  \"A set entry is `[path mark]`, a path vector and a mark of the profile's\n   kinds (a kind, or `[kind datum ...]`).\"\n  [entry]\n  (and (vector? entry)\n       (= 2 (count entry))\n       (vector? (first entry))\n       (let [mark (second entry)]\n         (boolean (some #{(if (vector? mark) (first mark) mark)}\n                        (:yang.site/kinds lowering-profile))))))\n\n\n(defn verify\n  \"Nil when the record at `record-address` verifies as the site set of the\n   module published at `manifest` in `handle`; `{:status :missing :address\n   a}` for the first address (manifest, record, set, in that order) the\n   handle does not hold; else the first defect `{:defect k ...}` (with the\n   record's `:set` address when it names one): the manifest or record not\n   its address, the record naming another manifest, the code root, the\n   frontend revision or the profile not the published ones, the set not its\n   address or empty, or an entry not a path with a mark kind of the\n   profile's.\"\n  [handle manifest record-address]\n  (let [m (read-at handle manifest)\n        r (when-not (absent? m) (read-at handle record-address))\n        set-address (when (map? r) (:yang.site/set r))\n        set (when-not (or (absent? m) (absent? r))\n              (read-at handle set-address))\n        defect (cond\n                 (not (map? m)) {:defect :manifest-absent}\n                 (not (map? r)) {:defect :record-absent}\n                 (not= manifest (:yang.site/manifest r)) {:defect :manifest}\n                 (not= (:yin.module/tree m) (:yang.site/root r)) {:defect :root}\n                 (not= frontend-revision (:yang.site/revision r))\n                 {:defect :revision}\n                 (not= profile-address (:yang.site/profile r)) {:defect :profile}\n                 (not (vector? set)) {:defect :set-absent}\n                 (empty? set) {:defect :set-empty}\n                 (not (every? entry-ok? set)) {:defect :kind}\n                 :else nil)]\n    (cond\n      (absent? m) (missing manifest)\n      (absent? r) (missing record-address)\n      (absent? set) (missing set-address)\n      defect (cond-> defect set-address (assoc :set set-address)))))\n\n\n(defn admit\n  \"A consumer's decision for the import of the module at `manifest` in\n   `handle`, `record-addresses` the publication records it discovered for\n   it, under `requirement`: `{:profile name :hooks true}` or `{:profile\n   name :noninterruptible true}`. Answers `{:status :ok :instrumented? true\n   :set [...] :set-address s :record a}` when a record verifies, `{:status\n   :ok :instrumented? false :note ...}` for a noninterruptible profile,\n   else a refusal naming the profile and the missing or mismatched set (its\n   address when the record names one); or `{:status :missing :address a}`,\n   the first address a record's verification reads that `handle` does not\n   hold (`verify`).\"\n  [handle manifest record-addresses requirement]\n  (cond\n    (:noninterruptible requirement)\n    {:status :ok,\n     :instrumented? false,\n     :note (str \"profile \" (pr-str (:profile requirement))\n                \" is noninterruptible: imported without a site set\")}\n\n    (:hooks requirement)\n    (if (empty? record-addresses)\n      (refusal :yang.python.antlr/missing-site-set requirement\n               \"no published site set for the module\" {:manifest manifest})\n      (loop [todo record-addresses\n             first-defect nil]\n        (if-let [a (first todo)]\n          (let [v (verify handle manifest a)]\n            (cond\n              (nil? v)\n              (let [set-address (:yang.site/set (fetch handle a))]\n                {:status :ok,\n                 :instrumented? true,\n                 :record a,\n                 :set-address set-address,\n                 :set (fetch handle set-address)})\n\n              (= :missing (:status v)) v\n\n              :else (recur (rest todo) (or first-defect [a v]))))\n          (let [[a defect] first-defect]\n            (refusal :yang.python.antlr/site-set-mismatch requirement\n                     (str \"the published site set\"\n                          (when-let [s (:set defect)] (str \" \" s))\n                          \" does not verify (\" (name (:defect defect)) \")\")\n                     (assoc defect :manifest manifest :record a))))))\n\n    :else\n    (refusal :yang.python.antlr/profile-undeclared requirement\n             \"the profile declares neither :hooks nor :noninterruptible\"\n             {:manifest manifest})))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Link-time application\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- load-tree\n  \"The canonical row set `{:root :rows}` of the tree at `root` in `handle`,\n   every row fetched by its address, depth-first: `{:status :missing\n   :address a}` for the first row the handle does not hold, nil when a row\n   is not its address or no tree row.\"\n  [handle root]\n  (loop [todo [root]\n         rows {}]\n    (if-let [id (peek todo)]\n      (if (contains? rows id)\n        (recur (pop todo) rows)\n        (let [body (read-at handle id)\n              slots (when (vector? body)\n                      (get vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar (first body)))]\n          (cond\n            (absent? body) (missing id)\n            slots (recur (into (pop todo)\n                               (mapcat (fn [[_ kind] v]\n                                         (case kind\n                                           :node [v]\n                                           :nodes v\n                                           nil))\n                                       slots\n                                       (rest body)))\n                         (assoc rows id (into [id] body)))\n            :else nil)))\n      {:root root, :rows rows})))\n\n\n(defn- derive-module\n  \"Publish into `handle` the instrumented module `A'` of the module `m` at\n   `manifest` for the verified set of `adm`: the published tree with the\n   hooks of `(:hooks-profile requirement)` at its sites, requiring\n   `(:pysp requirement)` beside its own requirements, each guest\n   requirement rebound through `derived` ({A A'}). The `:derive` record\n   (`yang.safepoint/derive-record`) is materialized beside it and its\n   address answered as `:derivation`; nothing transacts it into a ledger.\n   Answers `{:status :ok :address a' :derivation d}`, a refusal, or\n   `{:status :missing :address a}` for the first tree row the handle does\n   not hold, before anything is written.\"\n  [handle registry manifest m adm requirement derived]\n  (let [fail (fn [message data]\n               (refusal :yang.python.antlr/instrumentation-failed requirement\n                        message (assoc data :manifest manifest)))]\n    (if-not (jing/segment-address? (:pysp requirement))\n      (fail \"the hook module is not published\" {})\n      (try\n        (let [tree (load-tree handle (:yin.module/tree m))\n              absent (= :missing (:status tree))\n              sites (into {} (:set adm))\n              inserted (when (and tree (not absent))\n                         (safepoint/insert tree sites\n                                           (:hooks-profile requirement)))\n              ast (when inserted\n                    (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast (:tree inserted)))\n              runtime (or (:runtime requirement) '#{py pysp yang.call})\n              out (when ast\n                    (publish/publish-module!\n                      handle\n                      {:name (:yin.module/name m),\n                       :ast ast,\n                       :exports (:yin.module/exports m),\n                       :requires (assoc (into {}\n                                              (map (fn [[n a]]\n                                                     [n (if (contains? runtime n)\n                                                          a\n                                                          (get derived a a))]))\n                                              (:yin.module/requires m))\n                                        'pysp (:pysp requirement)),\n                       :primitives (into {}\n                                         (map (fn [[n _]]\n                                                [n (lower/primitive-declaration\n                                                     registry n)]))\n                                         (:yin.module/primitives m))}))]\n          (cond\n            absent tree\n            (nil? tree) (fail \"the module's tree is not published\" {})\n            (= :refused (:status out))\n            (fail \"the instrumented module did not publish\" {:cause out})\n            :else\n            {:status :ok,\n             :address (:address out),\n             :instrumented? true,\n             :set-address (:set-address adm),\n             :derivation (jing/materialize!\n                           handle\n                           (safepoint/derive-record\n                             (:yin.module/tree m)\n                             (:yin.module/tree (:manifest out))\n                             (:hooks-profile requirement)\n                             (:sites inserted)))}))\n        (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\n          (fail (str \"the instrumented module did not derive: \" (ex-message e))\n                {}))))))\n\n\n(defn- records-for\n  \"The record addresses for `manifest`: the local map `records`'s, then\n   those `ctx`'s loaded index datoms publish under its principals.\"\n  [ctx records manifest]\n  (into (vec (get records manifest))\n        (discover (:datoms ctx) (:principals ctx) manifest)))\n\n\n(defn- derive-closure\n  \"The instrumented module of `manifest` under a hook profile, derived\n   bottom-up: each guest requirement is admitted and derived first, so the\n   derived module pins the derived requirements it will link (`derived`,\n   {A A'}, derives a shared requirement once). Answers `{:status :ok\n   :address a' :derivation d :derived derived}`, the first refusal, with\n   `:name` the requirement that was refused and `:via` the module that\n   required it, or `{:status :missing :address a}` for the first address\n   (record, set, guest manifest, tree row) read in a fixed depth-first\n   order that the handle does not hold. Nothing is written until every\n   read has succeeded.\"\n  [ctx registry records requirement manifest derived]\n  (let [handle (:store ctx)\n        runtime (or (:runtime requirement) '#{py pysp yang.call})\n        adm (admit handle manifest (records-for ctx records manifest)\n                   requirement)]\n    (if (not= :ok (:status adm))\n      adm\n      (let [m (fetch handle manifest)\n            deps (sort-by (comp str key)\n                          (remove (comp runtime key)\n                                  (:yin.module/requires m)))\n            below (reduce (fn [acc [n a]]\n                            (if (contains? (:derived acc) a)\n                              acc\n                              (let [r (derive-closure ctx registry records\n                                                      requirement a\n                                                      (:derived acc))]\n                                (case (:status r)\n                                  :ok (assoc acc :derived (:derived r))\n                                  :missing (reduced r)\n                                  (reduced (cond-> (assoc r :via manifest)\n                                             (nil? (:name r)) (assoc :name n)))))))\n                          {:derived derived}\n                          deps)]\n        (if (:status below)\n          below\n          (let [out (derive-module handle registry manifest m adm requirement\n                                   (:derived below))]\n            (if (not= :ok (:status out))\n              out\n              (assoc out :derived (assoc (:derived below) manifest\n                                         (:address out))))))))))\n\n\n(defn- overlay\n  \"A write-buffering handle over `base`: reads see the buffer first, then\n   `base`; puts land in the buffer only, in put order. `flush!` writes the\n   buffer to `base`; dropping the overlay writes nothing.\"\n  [base]\n  (let [buf (atom {:bytes {}, :order []})\n        sentinel #?(:cljs (js-obj)\n                    :default (Object.))]\n    {:handle (assoc base\n                    :get-bytes-fn (fn [address not-found]\n                                    (let [bs (get-in @buf [:bytes address])]\n                                      (if (nil? bs)\n                                        ((:get-bytes-fn base) address not-found)\n                                        bs)))\n                    :put-bytes-fn (fn [address bs]\n                                    (if (or (contains? (:bytes @buf) address)\n                                            (let [existing ((:get-bytes-fn base)\n                                                            address\n                                                            sentinel)]\n                                              (and (some? existing)\n                                                   (not (identical? sentinel\n                                                                    existing)))))\n                                      :present\n                                      (do (swap! buf (fn [b]\n                                                       (-> b\n                                                           (assoc-in [:bytes address]\n                                                                     bs)\n                                                           (update :order conj\n                                                                   address))))\n                                          :inserted))))\n     :flush! (fn []\n               (let [{:keys [bytes order]} @buf]\n                 (doseq [a order] ((:put-bytes-fn base) a (get bytes a)))))}))\n\n\n(def reasons\n  \"The refusal reasons the stage answers, the composition's own.\"\n  #{:yang.python.antlr/missing-site-set\n    :yang.python.antlr/site-set-mismatch\n    :yang.python.antlr/profile-undeclared\n    :yang.python.antlr/instrumentation-failed})\n\n\n(defn stage\n  \"The link-time stage (`yin.vm.linker.interpreter/composition`'s `:stage`)\n   of a Python import under `requirement`: `{:profile name :hooks true\n   :hooks-profile p :pysp address}` or `{:profile name :noninterruptible\n   true}`, with `:runtime` the names of the runtime modules (default `py`,\n   `pysp`, and `yang.call`) it passes through. `yang.call` is the shared\n   call dispatcher, not guest Python code, so it has no Python site marks.\n   `records` ({manifest [record-address ...]})\n   are the local publication's records; a DHT source supplies none and the\n   stage discovers them from the index datoms of its context. The stage\n   reads only the context's `:store` and never fetches: it answers\n   `{:status :missing :address a}` for the first address that store does\n   not hold, and is run again once it is there. Answers\n   `(fn [ctx name address])`: `{:status :ok :address address}` as published\n   when noninterruptible, else the address of the instrumented module\n   derived bottom-up from the verified sets (`:derivation` its `:derive`\n   record's address), or the refusal naming the profile, the set and the\n   `:name` refused.\"\n  [registry requirement records]\n  (let [runtime (or (:runtime requirement) '#{py pysp yang.call})]\n    (fn [ctx name address]\n      (cond\n        (contains? runtime name) {:status :ok, :address address}\n\n        (:noninterruptible requirement)\n        (assoc (admit (:store ctx) address [] requirement) :address address)\n\n        :else\n        (let [{:keys [handle flush!]} (overlay (:store ctx))\n              r (derive-closure (assoc ctx :store handle) registry records\n                                requirement address {})]\n          (case (:status r)\n            :ok (do (flush!)\n                    (select-keys r [:status :address :derivation :instrumented?\n                                    :set-address]))\n            :missing (select-keys r [:status :address])\n            (cond-> r (nil? (:name r)) (assoc :name name))))))))\n\n\n(defn signed-entries\n  \"The `publisher` key's signed publication entries for the site sets\n   `publish-units!` answered: `sites` (`:sites`), the manifest of each\n   from `addresses` (a Python name) or `bundles` (a bundle symbol), in\n   key order. The publisher that asserts these names indexes them in the\n   same transaction (`entry-datoms`); a module with no entry is refused\n   by every hook-profile reader.\"\n  [publisher sites addresses bundles]\n  (mapv (fn [[k s]]\n          (signed-entry publisher (or (get addresses k) (get bundles k))\n                        (:record-address s)))\n        (sort-by (comp str key) sites)))\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:1486:  (let [response (support/linked-response (support/lit 42) 'pym.k #{'f})\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:1494:                            (support/v 'pym.k/f))))\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:1496:    (is (= {'pym.k (jing/segment-key manifest)} (:yin.k/modules requires)))\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:603:        yang.call/eligible?\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:661:        yang.call/invoke\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:1229:       [yang.call/eligible? true]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:1230:       [yang.call/invoke true]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:5571:        [yang.call/eligible? true]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:5572:        [yang.call/invoke true]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:7990:        pym.k/body\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:8001:        pym.k/spec},\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:8017:       [pym.k/spec true]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:8018:       [pym.k/body true]],\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:8043:        [pym.k/spec true]\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc:8044:        [pym.k/body true]]}},\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_evidence.edn:82:{:recorded-deviation {:boundary :nonportable-host-value, :upstream-value clojure.core/meta, :evidence {:file \"slice9_evidence.edn\", :fixture-id :elab-after-qed, :path [:a :outcome :thrown :data :meta]}, :reason \"Upstream embeds a host function in error data; the guest carries its closure. No agreement is claimed.\"}, :negative? true, :call (latte-kernel.proof/elab-proof env [] (quote [[:qed (lambda [A :type] (lambda [x A] x)) {}] [:qed missing {}]])), :agreement {:a-b true, :a-c-walker false, :a-c-semantic false, :c-lanes false}, :mode nil, :c-semantic {:status :completed, :outcome [:yin.k/err